msgid "The Keycloak group tree behind these paths is materialised by a per-application group-ldap-mapper (roles/web-app-keycloak/tasks/update/03_per_app_mappers.yml), anchored at `/<group_root>/<application_id>` with `group.name.ldap.attribute=description`."
msgstr ""

# translated-by: libretranslate
msgid "The Keycloak group tree is built by **per-application** `group-ldap-mapper`s. Each deployed application gets one mapper anchored at `/roles/<application_id>` whose LDAP filter `(&(objectClass=groupOfNames)(cn=<application_id>-*))` surfaces only that application's role groups. The mapper uses the LDAP `description` attribute as the visible Keycloak group name, so the resulting group paths carry no redundant prefix."
msgstr "The Keycloak nga **për** `group-ldap-mapper`në `/roles/<application_id>` filtro `(&(objectClass=groupOfNames)(cn=<application_id>-*))` Shfaq vetëm grupet e rolit të këtij aplikimi. Harta përdor LDAP `description` karakteristika i dukshëm Keycloak Emri i grupit, kështu që rrugët e grupit që rezultojnë nuk kanë parafiks të tepërt."

msgid "The Keycloak username pattern accepts both forms; the OpenLDAP length constraint becomes `IAM_ACCOUNT_MAX_LENGTH`. Only the provisioner creates names that contain `0`; the deploy and the reaper disable and report every account of the agent form without a provisioner record."
msgstr ""

msgid "The LDAP `filter_string` has no Playwright test for admitting a member on the native login form or through its administrator branch: with `sso` off no Keycloak exists to write a group membership to the directory, and the trusted-header bridge evaluates the filter only when it provisions an account, which `test-rbac-gate.js` spends on the user-group branch; options: an LDAP write helper in the persona library, or a second test persona."
msgstr ""

msgid "The LDAP federation sets ``trustEmail: true``: a user the federation imports for the first time is marked verified, because the directory is the platform’s own and seeded from the inventory. An entry an LDAP tool (LAM, phpLDAPadmin) creates with an address is trusted the same way. Keycloak applies it only at the first import, so every deploy with LDAP and ``verifyEmail`` extends the same trust to the users imported earlier: before the settings converge turns ``verifyEmail`` on, ```files/python/email_verified.py`` <files/python/email_verified.py>`__ with ``FEDERATION`` marks every user linked to the realm’s LDAP provider with a non-blank address verified (``tasks/update/06_users/email_federated.yml``). Local users without that link stay as they are, because their unverified state is what ``verifyEmail`` exists to catch. Inventory users are also marked verified at every deploy while ``services.keycloak.users.emails_verified`` is on (the default); any other local user confirms its address once through the link."
msgstr ""

msgid "The LDAP federation sets `trustEmail: true`: a user the federation imports for the first time is marked verified, because the directory is the platform's own and seeded from the inventory. An entry an LDAP tool (LAM, phpLDAPadmin) creates with an address is trusted the same way. Keycloak applies it only at the first import, so every deploy with LDAP and `verifyEmail` extends the same trust to the users imported earlier: before the settings converge turns `verifyEmail` on, [`files/python/email_verified.py`](files/python/email_verified.py) with `FEDERATION` marks every user linked to the realm's LDAP provider with a non-blank address verified (`tasks/update/06_users/email_federated.yml`). Local users without that link stay as they are, because their unverified state is what `verifyEmail` exists to catch. Inventory users are also marked verified at every deploy while `services.keycloak.users.emails_verified` is on (the default); any other local user confirms its address once through the link."
msgstr ""

msgid "The LDAP variant's group mapping (`web-app-jenkins-<role>` groups under the roles OU) is asserted only through the role-strategy assignment (`rbac: the LDAP realm grants each Jenkins role to its directory group`), not by a member signing in. Cause: the LDAP-only variant deploys no Keycloak, so a spec has no path to put biber into a directory group. Options: give the Playwright runner an LDAP write path (an `ldapmodify` helper or the `web-app-lam` API) and add a developer-membership scenario for the LDAP realm."
msgstr ""

