# translated-by: libretranslate
msgid "[x] Rotation and disablement tests prove that the old credential is rejected, the new credential works, and no stale client entry retains access. The first two run on every deploy: the shared contract probe opens the surface with the declared credential and fails unless a deterministic read succeeds, and separately proves a wrong credential is refused a session. Every one of the eight enabled serving surfaces includes that probe, so neither half is asserted for some roles and assumed for the rest. The third is unit-tested per client, and only three clients can hold a stale entry at all, because Hermes and OpenClaw keep no server-side registration: their configuration is rendered from the run's own discovery snapshot, so there is nothing left behind to go stale. Flowise overwrites the entry's `authConfig` and Open WebUI the registered connection, both already covered; n8n was the gap and now proves that a rotated bearer replaces the credential rather than being compared against one n8n never hands back, on the server side and for every client credential. Auditing the third half turned up a real leak on the way. Nextcloud mints an app password under a fixed managed name and never revoked the previous one, so every run that found the stored password rejected added another valid token to the account while the old ones kept full access. The mint is now preceded by a listing and the revocation of every token carrying that managed name."
msgstr ""

